+# the closed walks of the marked graph on the channels, forward along a channel and back along its acknowledge,
+# turning at joins and forks, with every loop's entry and exit channels cut.  A strongly connected component of that
+# walk graph that holds a back edge is a loop ring (one without is a fork / join diamond of code that runs once per
+# call); rings that share a node are one group.  A group's instances: its nodes' (fused cells included) and the links
+# on its channels.  A loop's directed cycles alone are not enough: xorshift's rounds (mux1, the xors, cond_br7) and its
+# counter (the compare, the steers, the merge) are two directed components, and the critical cycle of its routes runs
+# through both, joined by the select link n12__1 and by acknowledges.  The constraint goes on the kernel module as one
+# attribute,
+#     (* BDC_RING_PLACE = "w=K;<instances of group 0>;<instances of group 1>;..." *)
+# which survives synthesis (the module is keep_hierarchy) and which cells/hw/rloc_stamp.py turns into PLACE_WEIGHT K
+# (patches/nextpnr-xilinx-place-weight.patch) on every handshake net that lies in one group: a net touching a control
+# LUT of a group's instance, other than a delay chain's interior, and no control LUT outside that group.  A matched
+# delay's own links stay at weight 1: weighting them would shorten the delay itself, which the rules size and which a
+# placement change must not shorten (CLAUDE.md "Never shorten a delay to hit a speed target").
+# BDC_RING_PLACE_WEIGHT=K (default 8) sets K.
+RING_PLACE = _auto.AUTO and os.environ.get("BDC_RING_PLACE", "0") == "1"
